AB  - Achieving precise compositional consistency in text-to-image generation remains a fundamental challenge, particularly in training-free settings. Existing methods often operate via a reactive paradigm, where attention maps are corrected post-generation, which often falls short in resolving deep-seated semantic entanglement. We propose the Unified Compositional Controller (UCC), a modular framework that enforces structured control through a proactive, source-level disentanglement mechanism. UCC leverages a Large Language Model (LLM) to parse complex prompts into structured spatial-semantic blueprints. To address attribute leakage at its source, we introduce Selective Semantic Fusion (SSF), a technique that reconstructs text representations to explicitly bind attribute embeddings to subject tokens during input encoding. Furthermore, we design Temperature-Adjusted Cross-Attention with Text-Bridge (TACA-TB) to mitigate guidance dilution in DiT architectures via dynamic attention sharpening and semantic alignment restoration in deeper layers. Experimental results on the COCO491 and COCO-MIG benchmarks demonstrate that UCC not only outperforms existing training-free baselines but also achieves performance competitive with training-based approaches. UCC provides robust, zero-shot control over object layout, count, and attributes while maintaining high aesthetic quality.
