AB  - Additive Manufacturing (AM) offers great design freedom, enabling the fabrication of geometrically complex, multi-material, multi-scale, and functionally optimized parts. However, it also introduces unique challenges regarding manufacturability constraints and complicated interdependencies among design, manufacturing process, and material properties. Recent advances in Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) offer new opportunities to enhance AM workflow through data-driven approaches. This paper presents a comprehensive review of GenAI in AM, examining how advances in visual generative models and large language models, ranging from adversarial, variational, and diffusion-based architectures to foundation models like GPT, are reshaping design generation, process intelligence, and knowledge-driven manufacturing workflows. By integrating AM-specific insights into the generation process, VGMs enable generative design for AM, future build status prediction, process monitoring image enhancement, and data synthesis for downstream model training. Meanwhile, the powerful reasoning capabilities of LLMs enable knowledge acquisition, text-guided script synthesis for design and executable code generation, and adaptive process optimization. The review systematically assesses model architectures, applications, and performance across AM workflows, highlighting current challenges in data availability, constraint integration, three-dimensional (3D) generation, multi-source information fusion, and benchmarking. Future research directions toward context-aware, physics-informed, unified, and trustworthy GenAI frameworks are discussed to advance material design and enable smart, adaptive AM systems.
