AB  - Kansei engineering is an art to quantify users’ affective requirements into design specifications. However, design demand consistency — aligning users Kansei needs with producer’s design goals — is not always achieved, raising the questions of “how to quantify the Kansei perception gap between users and the producer”, and “how to minimize it”. This study proposes a method integrating Large Language Model (LLM), Large Vision Model (LVM) and Diffusion Model (DM) to address this gap. LLM and LVM process user-generated content (UGC) to extract emotional intensity, psychological traits, and preferences, while DM generate visual content to visualize design solutions, forming a multimodal data-driven framework named LAD (Large-models Aided Design), and being applied to a BYD car model in the fierce competitive new energy vehicle market. Data of more than 40,000 users, 20 car models, 300,000 reviews, and 20,000 images are used for the multimodal semantic embedding via Transformer to construct the Kansei-USER and the Kansei-AUTO. The Kansei-DPO model optimizes design parameters using the L-BFGS-B algorithm, and the Kansei-RPG model generates visual renderings via the DM. The framework is empirically validated in the automotive domain, with promising potential for extension to other product categories. Results are acknowledged by practitioners, followed by a prototype LAD optimization system.
