AB  - Evacuation simulation is critical for building safety, yet traditional approaches demand extensive engineering expertise and pose high barriers to routine use. This paper presents ChatEvac, an intelligent agent powered by a Large Language Model (LLM) that automates the full pipeline of evacuation assessment from user-uploaded architectural floorplans. A Diffusion model segments spatial features and evacuation nodes, a Social Force Model (SFM) engine executes pedestrian simulations, and an LLM orchestrates the workflow through an interactive graphical user interface (GUI). To mitigate LLM hallucination in this safety-sensitive context, three complementary mechanisms are integrated: a Finite State Machine (FSM) that constrains the LLM to valid workflow transitions, improving adherence by 21.4% over an unconstrained baseline; a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) module that grounds recommendations in authoritative building code provisions, improving citation accuracy by 40.4%; and a human-in-the-loop verification checkpoint in the GUI that intercepts residual errors at module boundaries. Experiments demonstrate feature segmentation accuracy above 89%, evacuation dynamics matching established simulation tools, and LLM-generated recommendations that are workflow-consistent and code-compliant. The proposed framework establishes a hallucination-mitigated paradigm for deploying LLMs in automated performance-based evacuation design.
