AB  - Recent advances in text-guided image generation have enabled powerful editing capabilities, particularly with diffusion models. However, most existing methods are limited to single-instruction edits and often struggle to accommodate complex real-world editing needs involving multiple objects, attributes, and nuanced transformations. To address this gap, we introduce ReVisE (Reflective Visual Editing), a framework designed for multi-instruction image manipulation. Inspired by the dual-process theory of fast and slow thinking, ReVisE first decomposes complex instructions into semantically coherent sub-tasks using a large language model (LLM), akin to rapid intuitive judgment. It then performs step-wise, iterative edits in a reflective manner, ensuring both precision and semantic fidelity. To maintain visual consistency across editing stages, we incorporate a super-resolution module between steps, mitigating artifacts and drift. We further construct two instruction-centric datasets(https://github.com/Junyi136/Instruct-Edit), Instruct-CelebA and Instruct-ADE, to evaluate the effectiveness of ReVisE. Experiments across multiple metrics, including instruction-image alignment, editing accuracy, and region preservation, demonstrate that ReVisE significantly outperforms prior methods in handling complex, multi-target edits.
