AB  - Understanding how low-carbon policies diffuse across time, policy domains, and administrative jurisdictions is essential for improving climate governance. This study analyzes 8741 Chinese low-carbon policy documents issued between 2007 and 2024 and develops an LLM-based framework to map policy topics and examine topic-specific diffusion patterns. The topic modeling procedure identifies 12 policy topics grouped into three dimensions: strategic planning, governance mechanisms, and application domains. Validation using manually annotated samples shows that DeepSeek-V3 performs better than conventional topic modeling baselines, including LDA, BERTopic, and SeededLDA, across purity, adjusted Rand index, and normalized mutual information. The results reveal differentiated temporal trajectories across topics. In addition to classical S-shaped and reverse R-shaped patterns, several topics show approximately linear diffusion, which may reflect how sustained administrative planning and continuous policy supply generate steady policy accumulation under China's planning-oriented governance system. Spatially, vertical diffusion shows an “east high, west low” gradient, while horizontal diffusion forms a multinode network centered on Shanghai, Beijing, and Guangdong. By integrating LLM-based topic modeling with temporal and spatial diffusion analysis, this study advances a multitopic understanding of policy diffusion and provides empirical evidence for improving the coordination of China's low-carbon policy system.
