T1  - Language extinction and diversification: The two sides of the same coin
AU  - Gong, Tao
JO  - Physics of Life Reviews
VL  - 59
SP  - 199
EP  - 219
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/12/01/
SN  - 1571-0645
DO  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.plrev.2026.07.005
UR  - https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S157106452600059X
KW  - Lexical diffusion
KW  - Feature pool
KW  - Utterance selection
KW  - Iterated learning
KW  - Agent-based simulation
KW  - Pólya urn
KW  - Price equation
KW  - Large language models
AB  - Long abstract
Background
Language extinction is conventionally treated as a conservation crisis to be arrested, and language diversification as a historical explanandum to be reconstructed. The two phenomena are studied separately, by different communities, with different methods.
Thesis
This separation is theoretically incoherent. Extinction and diversification are reciprocal outcomes of a single evolutionary dynamic operating on four nested scales of linguistic organisation. Whether a given dynamic is labelled “extinction” or “diversification” depends on the observer’s reference frame, not on the mechanism.
Approach
I integrate lexical diffusion (Wang), utterance selection (Croft), feature-pool ecology (Mufwene), iterated learning (Kirby), agent-based simulation (Gong et al.), and the Extended Evolutionary Synthesis (Laland et al.). A worked numerical example, using a Pólya-urn model with the Price-equation decomposition, demonstrates that identical micro-dynamics produce divergence or replacement depending on a single pair of ecological parameters (inter-group contact × prestige alignment).
Predictions
Five falsifiable predictions follow, each with a concrete operationalisation, from symmetric sign-reversal of endangerment predictors in phylogenetic diversification data, to measurable Large Language Model (LLM) induced diversity collapse in post-2022 corpora.
Implications
The framework reframes revitalisation policy (target niche conditions, not feature prescription), Artificial Intelligence (AI) design (diversity as a reward signal, not an afterthought), and the conceptual status of endangered languages (ecologically reconstructible, not ontologically tragic).
Short abstract
Language extinction and language diversification are not opposing phenomena; they are the same evolutionary process running in two directions. A single multi-level selection framework that spans sub-lexical, feature-pool, population, and machine-mediated scales predicts both divergence and death, and a tractable Pólya-urn simulation shows how identical micro-dynamics flip between the two under one ecological parameter.
