AB  - The diffusion of novel ideas in science is frequently constrained by established actors, resulting in delayed recognition of innovative work. However, it remains unclear whether the theoretical, methodological, and results-based novelty of scientific articles, as retrospectively reported by authors, distinctly influences recognition delays and how the compositional characteristics of author teams moderate these effects. Accordingly, this study examines the correlation between different types of ex post commentary-based scientific novelty and recognition delays, while assessing how team size and the average academic age of team members moderate these relationships. Using 2565 Citation Classics articles and their corresponding commentaries, we identify ex post novelty types from author commentaries with large language models and quantify recognition delay through awakening time. The results indicate that theoretical and methodological novelty are associated with longer recognition delays, particularly the theoretical type, whereas results-based novelty is associated with earlier recognition. Importantly, the positive effect of methodological novelty appears to depend on the specific delayed-recognition metric used. Larger teams and those with higher average academic age are associated with expedited recognition, specifically for theoretical and results-based novelty. Subsequent analyses reveal that theoretical novelty typically extends recognition for papers situated at the upper end of the awakening-time distribution, methodological novelty exhibits latency at both ends of the distribution, and results-based novelty consistently accelerates recognition. These findings enhance our understanding of how retrospectively narrated scientific novelty relates to varied recognition among eventually highly cited papers.
