AB  - Open-set instruction based dexterous robotic manipulation serves as a foundation for Human-Robot Collaboration (HRC), enabling robots to understand human intent and interact within dynamic smart manufacturing environments. While recent Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models and hierarchical frameworks have significantly advanced semantic task planning, they often lack the geometric precision and physics-aware contact reasoning required for industrial tool manipulation. Existing methods typically rely on implicit mappings or sparse keypoint constraints, which often lead to structurally unstable grasps when handling open-set tools with complex geometries. These limitations often lead to misinterpretation of task instructions, imprecise object localization, and unstable manipulation behaviors, finally leading to task failure. To address these challenges, this paper introduces a VLM-PoseManip that integrates semantic-spatial reasoning, instructive 6D pose estimation, and geometry-guided manipulation optimization to achieve fine-grained and task-aware dexterous manipulations. By leveraging Large Language Models (LLMs) and Vision Language Models (VLMs) for semantic parsing and affordance localization, a diffusion model for category-level 6D pose estimation, and a multi-factor grasp quality evaluation for manipulation planning, where the estimated 6D poses are further consumed by inverse kinematics (IK) to yield interpretable and geometrically precise end-effector motions. Instruction-driven reasoning and dynamic task manipulations in a laboratory setting are used to evaluate the proposed framework, which demonstrates strong generalization across tool categories and various tasks. Notably, our method achieves a semantic execution accuracy of 92.0%, an average task success rate of 83.0%, and a mean completion time under 5.25 s, thereby advancing dexterous manipulations in complex scenarios within HRC.
