AB  - ABSTRACT
Lithium-ion batteries enable diverse energy storage applications and represent a critical technology for renewable energy integration. Lithium metal oxide (LMO), serving as a pivotal cathode material, requires accelerated development through quantitative structure-activity relationship (QSAR) analysis. However, traditional database construction methods remain labor-intensive and expertise-dependent. Here we develop the Automated Material Discovery Lens (AMDL), a multimodal AI-driven workflow leveraging large language models to autonomously extract material data from 4621 scientific publications with 96.47 % text-mining precision and 58.9 % image-mining precision, construct material databases, and establish machine learning-QSAR models for targeted property prediction. Model interpretability analysis identifies c-axis length and Ni/Mn stoichiometry as critical descriptors governing lithium-ion diffusion in LMOs, with validation through density functional theory calculations. This work reveals 73 materials with previously unconfirmed high lithium-ion diffusivity, demonstrating AMDL’s capacity to transform fragmented knowledge into actionable materials design principles transferable beyond battery systems.
