AB  - Generative Artificial Intelligence (GenAI) models, especially neural network-based architectures, have proven to be powerful tools to address the data imbalance problem and improve model robustness in fault diagnostics and prognostics of rotating machinery. This review discusses the advancement in key GenAI models in this domain over the last decade, focusing on Variational Autoencoders (VAEs), diffusion models, and Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs), with their variants. These GenAI models are widely adopted for generating synthetic data, thereby enhancing fault diagnostics and prognostics. Furthermore, the application of transformer models in a generative context for diagnostics with other GenAI models is also covered, along with their application in prognostics due to their strong capability in processing sequential data. The working and mathematical theories related to these models are discussed in detail, along with the existing limitations, challenges, and future research directions. Further, the prospects and ongoing development using Large Language Models (LLMs) in the field are also provided. So, by providing the theoretical basis, ongoing advancements, and outlining challenges with potential research pathways, this review paper aims to highlight how GenAI models can drive the development of data-efficient, adaptive, and reliable fault diagnostics and prognostics solutions for rotating machinery.
