AB  - Automated risk control measure generation is crucial for safety and reliability in complex engineering systems. Traditional expert-based methods are labor-intensive and subjective. While large language models (LLMs) exhibit strong general capabilities, their specialized-domain outputs lack reliability due to limited domain knowledge. To address these challenges, we propose a physics-informed multi-task generation (PIMTG) framework that integrates physical constraints of risk propagation with deep learning. PIMTG enables co-optimized generation of three risk control measures: engineering interventions, safety management strategies, and emergency preparedness protocols, enhancing both textual quality and physical rationality. Specifically, we first develop a gated fusion mechanism with adaptive attention for heterogeneous features (textual, numerical, categorical). Additionally, we propose a multi-task prefix adapter to generate different risk control measures. Finally, we develop a training objective incorporating risk energy and evolution constraint equations, jointly minimizing text generation loss and physical consistency loss. This ensures that risk source terms, diffusion terms, absorption terms, and inherent risk values comply with first principles. On real industrial cases, PIMTG achieves an average BERTScore of 78.75%, a physical consistency index of 99.9%, and a symmetric mean absolute percentage error (SMAPE) of 0.0448, demonstrating its accuracy and reliability in automated risk control.
