AB  - In computer vision, training on limited data often leads to weak generalization, as the model struggles to learn robust decision boundaries from a few samples. Data augmentation has proven crucial for mitigating this issue, with recent efforts leveraging diffusion models (DMs) to generate realistic synthetic data. However, simply prompting a DM with a class name risks producing misleading or irrelevant outputs (e.g., confusion from ambiguity or a lack of contextual details), while methods that fine-tune DMs on few samples can be computationally expensive and remain tightly coupled to the limited real data available. In this work, we propose an effective framework that integrates a large language model (LLM) with a DM through a controllable, two-type augmentation process. Specifically, we design diversity-focused synthesis, in which the LLM generates context-rich prompts that guide the DM under low reference guidance to yield diverse synthetic images, and key feature-focused synthesis, which emphasizes class-defining visual attributes using higher reference guidance to preserve fine-grained characteristics of the target class. These two complementary types of synthetic data are then incorporated into a two-stage training scheme that (1) broadens the downstream classifier’s representation and (2) refines it to align with key class-invariant features. We observe consistent improvements in one-shot learning across multiple classifier backbones and confirm the gains in additional low-shot evaluations.
