AB  - Social network simulation enables controlled study of information dynamics and collective behavior on social graphs, yet the literature remains fragmented across disciplines. This survey provides a unified treatment of the field, organizing 97 papers across seven decades into five paradigms: network structure models, information diffusion, opinion dynamics, rule-based agent-based models, and large language model (LLM)-based agentic simulation. We bridge classical and generative approaches by mapping the progression from analytical update rules to language-grounded agents with semantic memory and platform-native actions, and present a systematic architectural comparison of LLM-based simulation systems. We analyze the literature across five application domains—misinformation, political discourse, platform design, information diffusion, and user behavior—tracing a shift from descriptive analysis toward prescriptive policy prototyping. We outline seven research frontiers spanning representational alignment, multi-level validation, scalability, and responsible use. An accompanying online repository at https://github.com/tamlhp/awesome-sns catalogs 48 implementations (42 open-source), 21 benchmarks and datasets, and 22 evaluation metrics.
