AB  - Recommender systems are a core technology for mitigating information overload and have been widely deployed across e-commerce, short-video, and social media platforms. Although notable progress has been made in modeling user-interest sequences, challenges persist in long-tail recommendation and cold-start scenarios, where representations of long-tail items are often suboptimal and semantic information is prone to loss. To address these issues, a dual-channel enhanced recommendation model, Collaborative Denoising and Semantic Preservation (CDSP), is introduced to jointly leverage the collaborative and semantic spaces. Performance in long-tail and cold-start settings is improved through a Frequency-Aware Diffusion (FAD) module and a semantic projection network based on the Kolmogorov–Arnold Network (KAN), named KAN-Bridge. By incorporating item interaction frequency, FAD selectively repairs embeddings of long-tail items, thereby enhancing the modeling capacity for mid- and low-popularity items. During the dimensionality reduction of high-dimensional semantic embeddings, KAN-Bridge performs a nonlinear mapping with learnable functions, which preserves semantic structure and alleviates the information loss typical of conventional reduction methods. Experimental results show that CDSP outperforms strong baselines on multiple public datasets; on nDCG@10, improvements of 4.23%, 1.73%, and 13.86% are achieved on Prime Pantry, MIND, and Electronics, respectively. The code and datasets are available at https://github.com/ChenJazzyBoss/CDSP.
