AB  - Diffusion models (DMs) have become a dominant paradigm for text-to-image (T2I) generation, evolving from the foundational DDPM framework to advanced variants such as Stable Diffusion and flow-matching models. Despite their impressive visual quality, pretrained diffusion models often fail to align with human preferences, producing images with semantic mismatches to the prompt or unsatisfactory aesthetic outcomes. Motivated by the success of alignment methods in large language models, recent studies have begun integrating reward signals into the training or inference optimization of diffusion models. This paper presents a comprehensive review of T2I diffusion model human preference alignment. First, we summarize the fundamentals and recent advances in diffusion models, then provide a systematic review of existing alignment methods and benchmark the representative alignment methods. Next, we review preference construction pipelines, including preference datasets and evaluation metrics. Finally, we discuss open challenges and outline promising future research directions to support more controllable and reliable aligned image generation.
