AB  - Safe navigation, planning, and interaction in real-world environments are important engineering applications of artificial intelligence, motivating the use of large language models (LLMs) for geometric reasoning over three-dimensional (3D) scenes. Unlike two-dimensional images, 3D environments encode rich spatial structure, making accurate geometric reasoning essential for tasks like question answering, planning, navigation, and human-aligned interaction. Current approaches rely on shallow, single-step projections to map 3D features into LLM prompts-methods that struggle to fully bridge the nonlinear gap between structured geometry and the linguistic priors of frozen LLMs, particularly for compositional spatial reasoning. We propose a diffusion-based refinement framework for bridging 3D geometry and text prompts compatible with frozen large language models (DiffBridge3D), which explicitly models and corrects the misalignment between structured geometry and language priors. Specifically, we formulate this misalignment as structured noise in the prompt space. We further introduce a Conditional Diffusion Bridge that progressively denoises tokens derived from 3D features into prompts aligned with the effective text-prompt distribution of the frozen LLMs. The framework is trained end-to-end using answer-optimized targets selected from a per-sample prompt bank, without modifying the frozen LLMs. Experimental results on five 3D vision-language benchmarks demonstrate that DiffBridge3D consistently outperforms existing methods. Our code is available at https://github.com/junhui661/DiffBridge3D.
