AB  - Task-oriented grasping requires both physical stability and semantic consistency. Part-based methods enhance TOG performance by analyzing object parts. However, they rely on predefined part labels and large-scale annotated datasets, which limit scalability and generalization. Alternatively, some rely on LLM-driven 2D and linguistic cues, which lack full 3D awareness and produce coarse, error-prone part proposals. In this paper, we propose PartDexTOG, a method that marries the semantic reasoning capabilities of large language models with part-based geometry analysis to generate dexterous task-oriented grasps. Taking a 3D object and a manipulation task represented by language as input, our method first generates category-level and part-level grasp descriptions w.r.t the manipulation task using LLMs and part-based geometry analysis. Then, a conditional diffusion model is developed to generate a dexterous grasp for each valid part, respectively, based on the generated descriptions. To select the most plausible combination of grasp and corresponding part from the generated ones, we propose a measure of geometric consistency between grasp and part. We show that our method greatly benefits from integrating LLMs’ semantic reasoning about object parts with language-geometry consistency analysis, which naturally generalizes to diverse object geometries and manipulation tasks and enables robust, task-oriented grasp generation without large-scale annotated datasets. Our method ranks top on the OakInk-shape dataset over all previous methods, improving the Penetration Volume, the Grasp Displace, and the P-FID over the state-of-the-art by 3.58%, 2.87%, and 41.43%, respectively. It also demonstrates excellent performance in real-world experiments, alongside strong generality capabilities for novel object categories and tasks.
